
  <sn><md>### Escaping Special Characters in Regex

Some characters have special meaning in a regex. When you want one of them to match as plain text, you put a backslash `\` in front of it. The backslash tells the engine: "treat the next character literally."

```
Pattern:  3\.14
Text:     "3.14 and 3x14"
Match:    "3.14" only
```

Without the backslash, the dot is a wildcard and would also match "3x14".
</md></sn>
